name: integrity
boss: Collision
flavor: Your hashes are already broken.
brief: A hash turns any amount of data into a short fixed string. Change one
brief: bit anywhere and the hash changes completely. That is the whole idea,
brief: and it is what this entire operating system is built on.
brief:
brief: Use sha256sum. md5sum and sha1sum still exist because old systems
brief: published those, and you will need to check them -- but neither is safe
brief: against someone deliberately constructing a collision. For "did this
brief: file arrive intact" they are fine. For "did an attacker swap it", they
brief: are not.
---
q: compute the sha256 of @txt@
a: sha256sum @txt@
out: ^[0-9a-f]{64}
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
---
q: compute the sha256 of @txt@ reading it from standard input, so no filename is printed
a: sha256sum < @txt@
out: ^[0-9a-f]{64}
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
---
q: print only the hash of @txt@, with the filename cut away
a: sha256sum @txt@ | awk '{print $1}'
out: ^[0-9a-f]{64}$
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
---
q: save the hashes of every .log file here into sums.txt
a: sha256sum *.log > sums.txt
state: exists:sums.txt
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
---
q: check the files listed in sums.txt still match their recorded hashes
a: sha256sum *.log > sums.txt && sha256sum -c sums.txt
out: OK
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum -c
---
q: check sums.txt quietly, printing nothing unless something fails
a: sha256sum *.log > sums.txt && sha256sum -c -s sums.txt
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum -s
---
q: compute the sha512 of @txt@
a: sha512sum @txt@
out: ^[0-9a-f]{128}
ref: sha512sum
teach: sha512sum
---
q: verify a sha512 checksum file
a: sha512sum @txt@ > s5.txt && sha512sum -c s5.txt
out: OK
ref: sha512sum
teach: sha512sum -c
---
q: check a sha512 list without printing the successes
a: sha512sum @txt@ > s5.txt && sha512sum -c -s s5.txt
ref: sha512sum
teach: sha512sum -s
---
q: compute the sha1 of @txt@, for checking against an old published value
a: sha1sum @txt@
out: ^[0-9a-f]{40}
ref: sha1sum
teach: sha1sum
---
q: verify a sha1 checksum file
a: sha1sum @txt@ > s1.txt && sha1sum -c s1.txt
out: OK
ref: sha1sum
teach: sha1sum -c
---
q: check a sha1 list silently
a: sha1sum @txt@ > s1.txt && sha1sum -c -s s1.txt
ref: sha1sum
teach: sha1sum -s
---
q: compute the md5 of @txt@
a: md5sum @txt@
out: ^[0-9a-f]{32}
ref: md5sum
teach: md5sum
---
q: verify an md5 checksum file
a: md5sum @txt@ > m.txt && md5sum -c m.txt
out: OK
ref: md5sum
teach: md5sum -c
---
q: check an md5 list silently
a: md5sum @txt@ > m.txt && md5sum -c -s m.txt
ref: md5sum
teach: md5sum -s
---
q: compute the old unix checksum and byte count of @txt@
a: cksum @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: cksum
teach: cksum
---
q: are @txt@ and @txt2@ byte for byte identical
a: cmp @txt@ @txt2@
ref: cmp
teach: cmp
---
q: compare two files reporting only the exit status, printing nothing
a: cmp -s @txt@ @txt2@
ref: cmp
teach: cmp -s
---
q: list every differing byte between two files, not just the first
a: cmp -l @txt@ @txt2@
ref: cmp
teach: cmp -l
---
q: compare only the first 10 bytes of two files
a: cmp -n 10 @txt@ @txt2@
ref: cmp
teach: cmp -n
---
q: show the differences between @txt@ and @txt2@ line by line
a: diff @txt@ @txt2@
ref: diff
teach: diff
---
q: show the differences in the unified format patches use
a: diff -U 3 @txt@ @txt2@
ref: diff
teach: diff -U
---
q: report only WHETHER two files differ, not how
a: diff -q @txt@ @txt2@
ref: diff
teach: diff -q
---
q: compare two files ignoring case
a: diff -i @txt@ @txt2@
ref: diff
teach: diff -i
---
q: compare two files ignoring differences in whitespace
a: diff -w @txt@ @txt2@
ref: diff
teach: diff -w
---
q: compare two directories recursively
a: diff -r @dir@ @dir2@
ref: diff
teach: diff -r
---
q: encode @txt@ as base64
a: base64 @txt@
out: [A-Za-z0-9+/]
ref: base64
teach: base64
---
q: decode a base64 file back to its original bytes
a: base64 @txt@ > b64.txt && base64 -d b64.txt
out: rotate
ref: base64
teach: base64 -d
hint: base64 is an encoding, not encryption -- anyone can undo it
---
q: encode @txt@ as base64 wrapped at 40 columns
a: base64 -w 40 @txt@
out: [A-Za-z0-9+/]
ref: base64
teach: base64 -w
